    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
            Why Big Data Matters for Modern Businesses
        </h2>

        <p>
            Businesses generate and collect data through websites, applications,
            customer interactions, transactions, connected devices, and internal
            operational systems. This information can reveal valuable patterns
            about customer behavior, market trends, business performance, and
            operational efficiency.
        </p>

        <p>
            However, collecting data does not automatically create business
            value. When information is distributed across disconnected systems,
            stored in inconsistent formats, or analyzed manually, organizations
            may struggle to identify relevant insights and respond quickly to
            changing conditions.
        </p>

        <p>
            Big Data technologies help organizations process and analyze large,
            diverse, and rapidly generated datasets. By combining scalable data
            infrastructure, analytics, and appropriate governance, businesses
            can transform raw information into evidence that supports better
            decisions and more effective strategies.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">What Is Big Data?</h2>

        <p>
            Big Data refers to datasets whose size, complexity, diversity, or
            generation speed can make traditional data processing approaches
            insufficient. These datasets may include structured information from
            databases, semi-structured records such as JSON documents, and
            unstructured content such as text, images, audio, and video.
        </p>

        <p>
            Big Data is commonly described through five characteristics: volume,
            velocity, variety, veracity, and value. Volume represents the amount
            of information collected, velocity refers to how quickly it is
            generated and processed, and variety describes the different formats
            and sources involved.
        </p>

        <p>
            Veracity concerns data quality and reliability, while value
            represents the practical benefits organizations can obtain from
            analyzing their information. These characteristics help businesses
            understand the challenges involved in building effective data
            platforms.
        </p>

        <p>
            Big Data is not simply about storing more information. Its purpose
            is to make data accessible, trustworthy, and useful for solving
            business problems, improving products, and identifying opportunities
            that might otherwise remain hidden.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
            Collect and Integrate Data from Multiple Sources
        </h2>

        <p>
            Reliable analytics begins with access to relevant information.
            Businesses often need to combine data from customer relationship
            management systems, enterprise resource planning platforms,
            e-commerce applications, financial systems, marketing tools, and
            external services.
        </p>

        <p>
            Data integration brings these sources together so that teams can
            analyze information across business functions. APIs, database
            connectors, event streams, and file-based ingestion processes can
            help move information into a centralized data environment.
        </p>

        <p>
            Depending on business requirements, data may be processed in
            scheduled batches or continuously through streaming pipelines. Batch
            processing is often suitable for periodic reporting and historical
            analysis, while streaming can support use cases that require
            low-latency insights.
        </p>

        <p>
            A well-designed ingestion strategy accounts for source reliability,
            data formats, duplicate records, schema changes, and processing
            failures. These considerations help maintain consistent datasets
            that can support dependable analysis.
        </p>
    </section>

